Drop the clang diagnostic pragma around ZSTD_defaultCMem.

zstd.h wraps the ZSTD_defaultCMem initialiser in
`#pragma clang diagnostic ignored "-Wzero-as-null-pointer-constant"`, a
warning that exists only for C++ (NULL is a null pointer constant in C).
R CMD check reports any `#pragma (GCC|clang) diagnostic ignored` under
src/ as a NOTE ("pragma(s) suppressing diagnostics"), and CRAN's automated
pretest archives a submission on a NOTE, so the pragma cannot ship.

zukomp compiles zstd as C and never installs zstd.h, so no translation unit
that sees this header is C++ and the suppressed diagnostic cannot fire.
Removing the push/ignored/pop triple changes no code: the initialiser is
byte-for-byte what upstream compiles.

--- a/zstd.h
+++ b/zstd.h
@@ -1873,14 +1873,7 @@
 __attribute__((__unused__))
 #endif
 
-#if defined(__clang__) && __clang_major__ >= 5
-#pragma clang diagnostic push
-#pragma clang diagnostic ignored "-Wzero-as-null-pointer-constant"
-#endif
 ZSTD_customMem const ZSTD_defaultCMem = { NULL, NULL, NULL };  /**< this constant defers to stdlib's functions */
-#if defined(__clang__) && __clang_major__ >= 5
-#pragma clang diagnostic pop
-#endif
 
 ZSTDLIB_STATIC_API ZSTD_CCtx*    ZSTD_createCCtx_advanced(ZSTD_customMem customMem);
 ZSTDLIB_STATIC_API ZSTD_CStream* ZSTD_createCStream_advanced(ZSTD_customMem customMem);
